# United States: the holidays of the New York Stock Exchange, of the Federal Reserve and of the US bond market,
# the NYSE's early closes, and the dated events of US monetary policy and statistics (FOMC rate decisions, minutes,
# the Fed's communications blackout, CPI, GDP, personal income and outlays, the monthly jobs report, the New York Fed's
# Survey of Consumer Expectations, Treasury auctions).
#
# Translated from holidays-events rules/catalog/us.yaml.
#
# How to read a rule: "rule <key> for <event> in <scope> [valid from A to B] = <dates> with kind = ..., cites ...;".
# The part after "=" is a date expression evaluated for every year: "1 JAN" is the first of January, "3 MON JAN" the
# third Monday in January, "LAST MON MAY" the last Monday in May, "EASTER - 2D" two days before Western Easter Sunday.
# "{SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}" after a date moves it when it falls on a Saturday (one day back, to Friday) or a Sunday (one
# day forward, to Monday); a weekday not named there leaves the date where it is. "IF MON TUE WED THU" keeps a date
# only when it falls on one of those weekdays. "> NEXT WED" moves to the first Wednesday strictly after the date,
# "> PRECEDING" / "> FOLLOWING" to the business day before / after when the date is closed (on the calendar after
# "ON", else on the rule's own scope). "@key" stands for the dates of another rule. Rules only project: the dates an
# authority publishes are data that replace a rule's dates for that year, and dates that are only ever published have
# no rule. The kind says what the day does: "closed" (no trading or settlement), "short_day" (open with an early
# close; the session gives the hours), "event" (a dated event such as a decision or a release; the day stays a normal
# business day). Dates are inclusive. The full syntax is in README.md in this directory.
#
# Weekends: every scope here has the weekend Saturday and Sunday (see weekends.rules).
#
# Scopes used in this file:
#   exchange:XNYS          New York Stock Exchange (exchange, MIC XNYS), New York, America/New_York.
#   central_bank:FED       Federal Reserve (central bank of the United States). Its holidays are those of the
#                          Federal Reserve System's K.8 holiday schedule (the days Fedwire and the Reserve Banks
#                          are closed); the New York Fed's own survey releases are also filed here.
#   segment:US_BOND        US bond market (market segment): the days on which SIFMA, the Securities Industry and
#                          Financial Markets Association, recommends a full close of US dollar bond trading
#                          (ISDA business-day code USGS); US Treasury auctions are filed here too.
#   statistics_office:BLS  US Bureau of Labor Statistics (statistics office; consumer prices, jobs report).
#   statistics_office:BEA  US Bureau of Economic Analysis (statistics office; GDP, personal income and outlays).
#
# How the three holiday calendars differ (from the catalog's own comments):
#   - NYSE: a holiday on a Sunday is observed on the Monday after, a holiday on a Saturday on the Friday before,
#     EXCEPT New Year's Day: when 1 January is a Saturday the exchange does not close the Friday before (that
#     Friday is 31 December, the last trading day of the year). NYSE is open on Columbus Day and Veterans Day.
#   - Federal Reserve (K.8): adds Columbus Day and Veterans Day; a holiday on a Sunday is observed on the Monday
#     after, a holiday on a Saturday is NOT observed at all (no Friday closure). Good Friday is not a Fed holiday.
#   - US bond market (SIFMA): the Fed's holidays, but a holiday on a Saturday closes the Friday before (for example
#     Independence Day 2026: Friday 3 July "observed"), except New Year's Day (31 December is an early close, not a
#     closure). Good Friday is decided each year. These SIFMA rules were completed after a comparison with the USGS
#     calendar of the open-source library OpenGamma Strata.


# =====================================================================================================================
# exchange:XNYS - New York Stock Exchange (MIC XNYS)
# Who sets it: the NYSE publishes its holiday and early-close calendar (source nyse_holidays, "NYSE holidays &
# trading hours"). The holidays follow the US federal holidays, minus Columbus Day and Veterans Day, plus Good Friday.
# =====================================================================================================================

# New Year's Day, 1 January. The exchange is closed. If 1 January is a Sunday the exchange closes on Monday 2
# January instead. If it is a Saturday nothing moves: NYSE does not close on Friday 31 December, the last trading
# day of the year (an exception to its usual Saturday-to-Friday rule).
rule xnys.new_years_day for new_years_day in exchange:XNYS = 1 JAN {SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Martin Luther King Jr. Day: the third Monday in January (a US federal holiday since 1986). The NYSE has closed on
# it since 1998, so the rule starts with 1998.
rule xnys.mlk_day for mlk_day in exchange:XNYS valid from 1998-01-01 = 3 MON JAN
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Washington's Birthday (also called Presidents' Day): the third Monday in February. The exchange is closed.
rule xnys.washingtons_birthday for washingtons_birthday in exchange:XNYS = 3 MON FEB
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Good Friday: the Friday before Western Easter Sunday. Not a federal holiday, but the NYSE closes by its own rules.
rule xnys.good_friday for good_friday in exchange:XNYS = EASTER - 2D
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Memorial Day: the last Monday in May. The exchange is closed.
rule xnys.memorial_day for memorial_day in exchange:XNYS = LAST MON MAY
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Juneteenth National Independence Day, 19 June (a federal holiday since the Juneteenth National Independence Day
# Act of June 2021). The NYSE first closed for it in 2022, so the rule starts with 2022. On a Saturday the exchange
# closes the Friday before (18 June), on a Sunday the Monday after (20 June).
rule xnys.juneteenth for juneteenth in exchange:XNYS valid from 2022-01-01 = 19 JUN {SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Independence Day, 4 July. On a Saturday the exchange closes the Friday before (3 July), on a Sunday the Monday
# after (5 July).
rule xnys.independence_day for independence_day in exchange:XNYS = 4 JUL {SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Labor Day: the first Monday in September. The exchange is closed.
rule xnys.labor_day for labor_day in exchange:XNYS = 1 MON SEP
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Thanksgiving Day: the fourth Thursday in November. The exchange is closed.
rule xnys.thanksgiving for thanksgiving in exchange:XNYS = 4 THU NOV
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Christmas Day, 25 December. On a Saturday the exchange closes the Friday before (24 December), on a Sunday the
# Monday after (26 December).
rule xnys.christmas_day for christmas_day in exchange:XNYS = 25 DEC {SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# --- NYSE early closes: the exchange opens as usual at 09:30 New York time and closes early at 13:00 (the catalog
# --- gives the 13:00 close; 09:30 is the NYSE's regular opening time).

# The day after Thanksgiving (the Friday after the fourth Thursday in November): early close at 13:00.
rule xnys.day_after_thanksgiving_early_close for day_after_thanksgiving_early_close in exchange:XNYS
  = @xnys.thanksgiving + 1D
  with kind = short_day, session 09:30 - 13:00 tz "America/New_York"
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# Christmas Eve, 24 December: early close at 13:00, but only when 24 December is a Monday to Thursday. When it is a
# Friday the exchange is either closed for Christmas observed (Christmas on a Saturday) or trades a normal day; on a
# weekend there is no session anyway.
rule xnys.christmas_eve_early_close for christmas_eve_early_close in exchange:XNYS = 24 DEC IF MON TUE WED THU
  with kind = short_day, session 09:30 - 13:00 tz "America/New_York"
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;

# The day before Independence Day, 3 July: early close at 13:00, but only when 3 July is a Monday to Thursday (on a
# Friday it is the observed holiday when 4 July is a Saturday, or a normal day). The NYSE announces this early close
# each year, so these dates are a projection until the exchange confirms them.
rule xnys.independence_eve_early_close for independence_eve_early_close in exchange:XNYS = 3 JUL IF MON TUE WED THU
  with kind = short_day, session 09:30 - 13:00 tz "America/New_York",
       note "announced yearly by NYSE; a projection until confirmed"
  cites "NYSE holidays & trading hours" url "https://www.nyse.com/markets/hours-calendars" label official;


# =====================================================================================================================
# central_bank:FED - Federal Reserve
# Who sets it: the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System publishes the holiday schedule of the Federal
# Reserve Banks in its K.8 list (source fed_k8, "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays"). These are the eleven US federal
# holidays. A holiday on a Sunday is observed on the Monday after; a holiday on a Saturday is not observed, i.e. the
# Fed is open on the Friday before.
# =====================================================================================================================

# New Year's Day, 1 January. On a Sunday the Fed closes on Monday 2 January; on a Saturday nothing moves.
rule fed.new_years_day for new_years_day in central_bank:FED = 1 JAN {SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Martin Luther King Jr. Day: the third Monday in January.
rule fed.mlk_day for mlk_day in central_bank:FED = 3 MON JAN
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Washington's Birthday (Presidents' Day): the third Monday in February.
rule fed.washingtons_birthday for washingtons_birthday in central_bank:FED = 3 MON FEB
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Memorial Day: the last Monday in May.
rule fed.memorial_day for memorial_day in central_bank:FED = LAST MON MAY
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Juneteenth National Independence Day, 19 June. Legal basis: the Juneteenth National Independence Day Act, signed
# on 17 June 2021, which made it a federal holiday from that day (so 19 June 2021 already counts). On a Sunday the
# Fed closes the Monday after; on a Saturday (as in 2021) nothing moves.
rule fed.juneteenth for juneteenth in central_bank:FED valid from 2021-06-17 = 19 JUN {SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Independence Day, 4 July. On a Sunday the Fed closes the Monday after; on a Saturday nothing moves.
rule fed.independence_day for independence_day in central_bank:FED = 4 JUL {SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Labor Day: the first Monday in September.
rule fed.labor_day for labor_day in central_bank:FED = 1 MON SEP
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Columbus Day (also Indigenous Peoples' Day): the second Monday in October. A Fed holiday, while the NYSE is open.
rule fed.columbus_day for columbus_day in central_bank:FED = 2 MON OCT
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Veterans Day, 11 November. A Fed holiday, while the NYSE is open. On a Sunday the Fed closes the Monday after; on a
# Saturday nothing moves.
rule fed.veterans_day for veterans_day in central_bank:FED = 11 NOV {SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Thanksgiving Day: the fourth Thursday in November.
rule fed.thanksgiving for thanksgiving in central_bank:FED = 4 THU NOV
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# Christmas Day, 25 December. On a Sunday the Fed closes the Monday after (26 December); on a Saturday nothing moves.
rule fed.christmas_day for christmas_day in central_bank:FED = 25 DEC {SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Federal Reserve K.8 holidays" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm" label official;

# --- Monetary policy: the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) meets eight times a year; the source for all three
# --- rules below is the Fed's FOMC meeting calendar (source fed_fomc_calendar).

# FOMC rate decision: the day the FOMC announces its decision on the federal funds rate (the last day of a
# meeting, a Wednesday). The Fed publishes the meeting schedule for the next year in the middle of the year; those
# published dates are the authority. Beyond that schedule, three of the eight meetings follow a fixed pattern found
# in the Fed's own dates and are projected: the January, March and July decisions fall on the Wednesday on or after
# 26 January, 16 March and 26 July (January and July hit in every year 2014-2027, March in 13 of 14 years, 2017
# missed). The other five meetings move from year to year and have no projection: they appear only once announced.
# The statement is released at 2:00 pm Eastern time (the session).
# "25 JAN > NEXT WED" is the first Wednesday strictly after 25 January, i.e. on or after 26 January.
rule fed.fomc_decision for fomc_decision in central_bank:FED
  = 25 JAN > NEXT WED, 15 MAR > NEXT WED, 25 JUL > NEXT WED
  with kind = event, session 14:00 tz "America/New_York",
    note "schedule for the next year published mid-year; January, March and July projected"
  cites "FOMC meeting calendar" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm" label official;

# FOMC minutes: the minutes of each meeting are released three weeks (21 days) after its rate decision, so a
# Wednesday decision gives a Wednesday release. Follows every FOMC decision, announced or projected.
rule fed.fomc_minutes for fomc_minutes in central_bank:FED = @fed.fomc_decision + 21D
  with kind = event
  cites "FOMC meeting calendar" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm" label official;

# Fed communications blackout: the period around each FOMC meeting in which Fed officials do not speak in public
# about monetary policy. It starts on the second Saturday before the meeting's decision day and ends on the day
# after the decision (midnight Eastern Time). Every day of that span is an event day.
# The expression is one range per decision, from the second Saturday strictly before it ("> PREV 2ND SAT") to the
# day after it; the two ends are paired per occurrence of the FOMC rule.
rule fed.blackout for fed_blackout in central_bank:FED
  = [@fed.fomc_decision > PREV 2ND SAT : @fed.fomc_decision + 1D]
  with kind = event, note "second Saturday before the meeting until the day after it ends"
  cites "FOMC meeting calendar" url "https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm" label official;

# New York Fed Survey of Consumer Expectations: the monthly press release and data update of the Federal Reserve
# Bank of New York's household survey (the bank's own survey, not an official statistic). The New York Fed publishes
# the dates (without times) several months ahead in the calendar of its household surveys (source
# nyfed_microeconomics_calendar, a CSV behind the page). Beyond that, the pattern observed since May 2025 projects
# them: the 7th of each month, or the next business day of the Federal Reserve when the 7th is a weekend or Fed
# holiday (17 of 20 releases exactly, all 20 within one business day).
rule nyfed.consumer_expectations for nyfed_consumer_expectations in central_bank:FED
  = [7 JAN : 1M : 7 DEC] > FOLLOWING
  with kind = event, note "dates published several months ahead; projected as the 7th or the next business day"
  cites "New York Fed calendar of household surveys (Survey of Consumer Expectations)"
        url "https://www.newyorkfed.org/microeconomics/calendar" label official;


# =====================================================================================================================
# segment:US_BOND - US bond market (SIFMA recommendations)
# Who sets it: SIFMA, the Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association, publishes yearly recommendations for
# full and early closes of US dollar bond trading (source sifma_holidays, "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close
# recommendations"). The full closes are the Fed's holidays (including Columbus Day and Veterans Day, when the NYSE
# is open), but a holiday on a Saturday closes the Friday before, except New Year's Day (when 1 January is a
# Saturday, 31 December is an early close, not a closure). SIFMA's early closes are not modelled here.
# =====================================================================================================================

# New Year's Day, 1 January. On a Sunday the bond market closes on Monday 2 January. On a Saturday it does not close
# on Friday 31 December (SIFMA recommends an early close that day instead).
rule us_bond.new_years_day for new_years_day in segment:US_BOND = 1 JAN {SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Martin Luther King Jr. Day: the third Monday in January.
rule us_bond.mlk_day for mlk_day in segment:US_BOND = 3 MON JAN
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Washington's Birthday (Presidents' Day): the third Monday in February.
rule us_bond.washingtons_birthday for washingtons_birthday in segment:US_BOND = 3 MON FEB
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Good Friday: the Friday before Western Easter Sunday. SIFMA decides each year whether the bond market closes: a
# full close in most years, but only an early close (12:00 New York time) when the monthly employment report falls
# on Good Friday, as in 2026. So there is no rule: the date counts as a full close only once SIFMA's yearly
# recommendation (published months ahead) says so; until then no date is given.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (sifma_holidays); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# us_bond.good_friday.

# Memorial Day: the last Monday in May.
rule us_bond.memorial_day for memorial_day in segment:US_BOND = LAST MON MAY
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Juneteenth National Independence Day, 19 June, from 2022 (the first year SIFMA recommended a close). On a Saturday
# the bond market closes the Friday before, on a Sunday the Monday after.
rule us_bond.juneteenth for juneteenth in segment:US_BOND valid from 2022-01-01 = 19 JUN {SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Independence Day, 4 July. On a Saturday the bond market closes the Friday before (3 July, as in 2026), on a Sunday
# the Monday after.
rule us_bond.independence_day for independence_day in segment:US_BOND = 4 JUL {SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Labor Day: the first Monday in September.
rule us_bond.labor_day for labor_day in segment:US_BOND = 1 MON SEP
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Thanksgiving Day: the fourth Thursday in November.
rule us_bond.thanksgiving for thanksgiving in segment:US_BOND = 4 THU NOV
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Christmas Day, 25 December. On a Saturday the bond market closes the Friday before (24 December), on a Sunday the
# Monday after (26 December).
rule us_bond.christmas_day for christmas_day in segment:US_BOND = 25 DEC {SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Columbus Day: the second Monday in October. The bond market closes although the NYSE is open.
rule us_bond.columbus_day for columbus_day in segment:US_BOND = 2 MON OCT
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# Veterans Day, 11 November. The bond market closes although the NYSE is open. On a Saturday it closes the Friday
# before (10 November), on a Sunday the Monday after (12 November).
rule us_bond.veterans_day for veterans_day in segment:US_BOND = 11 NOV {SAT - 1D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "SIFMA holiday schedule and early-close recommendations"
        url "https://www.sifma.org/resources/general/holiday-schedule/" label official;

# US Treasury auction: the auctions of Treasury bills, notes, bonds and TIPS by the US Department of the Treasury.
# Each auction is announced a few days before it takes place; the Treasury's Fiscal Data service lists announced
# auctions about two weeks ahead (source us_treasury_auctions, the "upcoming auctions" dataset). No pattern is
# projected, so there are no dates until announced.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (us_treasury_auctions); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# ust.auction.


# =====================================================================================================================
# statistics_office:BLS - US Bureau of Labor Statistics
# Who sets it: the BLS publishes a yearly release schedule, also as an iCal feed (source bls_ical, "BLS release
# schedule (iCal)").
# =====================================================================================================================

# US Consumer Price Index (CPI) release: the monthly consumer-price report. The dates come only from the BLS's
# yearly schedule; no pattern is projected, so there are no dates until announced.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (bls_ical); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# bls.cpi.

# US Employment Situation (the monthly jobs report with nonfarm payrolls). The dates come from the BLS's yearly
# schedule. Beyond it they are projected from the BLS's own statement that the report is "typically released on the
# third Friday after the conclusion of the reference week", the reference week being the week (Sunday to Saturday)
# that includes the 12th of the previous month. Two observed adjustments: a January release that would fall on 1 to
# 3 January moves one week later, and a release on a closed day moves to the business day before. Holidays are
# taken from the Federal Reserve's calendar. Checked against the published dates for 2022-2027: 68 of 69 hits; the
# miss is 2 July 2026, because federal offices close on Friday 3 July 2026 (Independence Day on a Saturday) but the
# Fed, whose calendar is used here, does not.
# The expression: for February to December, take the 12th of the previous month (January to November), go to the
# Saturday on or after it ("- 1D > NEXT SAT": the first Saturday strictly after the 11th), add 20 days (the third
# Friday after that Saturday), and roll back to a Fed business day. For January the same steps give a Friday from 1
# to 7 January, and the early-January move turns 1-3 January into 8-10 January, so the January release is always
# the first Friday on or after 4 January ("3 JAN > NEXT FRI"), rolled back to a Fed business day.
rule bls.employment_situation for us_employment_situation in statistics_office:BLS
  = 3 JAN > NEXT FRI > PRECEDING ON central_bank:FED,
      [12 JAN : 1M : 12 NOV] - 1D > NEXT SAT + 20D > PRECEDING ON central_bank:FED
  with kind = event, note "projected as the third Friday after the reference week (the week including the 12th)"
  cites "BLS release schedule (iCal)" url "https://www.bls.gov/schedule/news_release/bls.ics" label official;


# =====================================================================================================================
# statistics_office:BEA - US Bureau of Economic Analysis
# Who sets it: the BEA publishes a yearly release schedule with the next year's tab already online (source
# bea_release_schedule, "BEA release schedule (GDP, personal income and outlays incl. PCE)"). Release times are
# 8:30, 10:00 or 13:30 Washington time.
# =====================================================================================================================

# US GDP release: the advance, second and third estimates of gross domestic product. The dates come only from the
# BEA's schedule; no pattern is projected, so there are no dates until announced.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (bea_release_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# bea.gdp.

# US Personal Income and Outlays, which includes the PCE price index (the Fed's preferred inflation measure). The
# dates come only from the BEA's schedule; no pattern is projected, so there are no dates until announced.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (bea_release_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# bea.personal_income_outlays.
